WebRTC: ask the cloud for a key frame (RTCP PLI) until we get one -- then
keep asking whenever the picture goes stale -- and count what happens.

extern/libpeer is a git SUBMODULE, so its sources must not be edited in
place: the next `git submodule update` would throw the changes away.  This
patch is applied to the checked out tree by
tools/apply-libpeer-switch-patches.sh, right before libpeer is built (it is
the eleventh patch of that series, after 0001-switch-webrtc-fixes,
0002-no-stdout-log, 0003-h265-depacketizer, 0004-dtls-diagnostics,
0005-ice-roles-prflx-and-timing, 0006-dtls-fingerprint-and-verify,
0007-ice-timeout-and-verify-none, 0008-dtls-bio-remb-and-candidate-dedup,
0009-sctp-init-and-diagnostics and 0010-hole-attribution).

Why
---

libpeer has had a PLI sender for ever:

  * rtcp_get_pli() builds a 12 byte RTCP PLI (src/rtcp.c);
  * peer_connection_send_rtcp_pil() encrypts it (SRTCP) and sends it;
  * peer_connection_request_video_keyframe() is the public entry point.

NONE of them had a caller.  The whole tree asked the cloud for a key frame
exactly ZERO times -- and the official client asks on a regular schedule
(the reverse engineering of the APK found "rtcp_packet/pli.cc",
"fir.cc", "MHYRtcPli ... requesting keyframe" and a "Pli interval:"
parameter; its decoder waits for an IDR and shows nothing without it:
"AndroidDecoder::waitKeyFrame").

Two things make the request necessary here:

  1. A STATIC picture makes the encoder produce no new IDR at all.  The PC
     capture of the very same session shows ~69 bytes per frame -- an
     all-skip P frame -- so there is nothing to decode unless somebody asks
     for a key frame.
  2. Even if the cloud pushes an IDR, a request is the repeatable, self
     healing way to get one.

What
---

  * A PLI timer in peer_connection_loop().  The rules:

      - only on a completed transport (the RTCP goes out over SRTCP);
      - ★ B2: while the last key frame is STALE -- a key frame that
        arrived within LIBPEER_PLI_STALE_MS (3 s) suppresses the request,
        an older one (or none at all) asks again.  The first version
        stopped after the FIRST key frame ("the official client stops
        asking as well") -- a premise that does not hold for us: the
        official client does not lose packets and never needs to ask
        again, while we lose packets on every capture and the cloud
        inserts no periodic IDR, so every loss after the first key frame
        corrupted the picture forever (the "yellow -> black -> yellow"
        loop);
      - the first request goes out LIBPEER_PLI_FIRST_DELAY_MS (500 ms)
        after the transport came up -- the cloud pushes an IDR when the
        stream starts, so the very first request would only race with it;
      - then one request every LIBPEER_PLI_INTERVAL_MS (300 ms) until a
        fresh key frame arrives.  The official client repeats too (200-500
        ms) -- this is a regular mechanism, not an emergency fallback;
      - at most one PLI per loop tick (12 bytes).

    Both constants can be overridden at compile time
    (-DLIBPEER_PLI_INTERVAL_MS=... / -DLIBPEER_PLI_FIRST_DELAY_MS=...) and
    are clamped (interval 100..5000 ms, first delay 0..5000 ms).

  * The media SSRC.  A PLI addresses a media SSRC, so it cannot be sent
    before we know one.  Two ways to learn it, both implemented:

      - the remote description: the cloud's offer carries
        "a=ssrc:2331692711 cname:..." in the video section, and
        peer_connection_set_remote_description() parses it into
        pc->remote_vssrc => the PLI can go out before the first RTP packet;
      - the first video RTP packet: if the description carried no SSRC,
        the first packet teaches us (pc->remote_vssrc == 0 branch) -- and
        that is the moment the first PLI is sent.

  * The counters (this is the part that makes the next report readable):

      - keyframes_received -- how many key frames the depacketizer saw.
        H.264: NAL type 5 (IDR).  H.265: IRAP, NAL types 16..23 (in
        practice 19 = IDR_W_RADL, 20 = IDR_N_LP, 21 = CRA).  The parameter
        sets (H.264 NAL 7 = SPS, 8 = PPS) are NOT key frames: they carry
        no picture, and "wait for a key frame" does not mean them.
      - last_keyframe_ms -- wall clock of the last key frame.
      - pli_sent -- how many PLIs went out.
      - pli_last_sent_ms -- wall clock of the last PLI.

    A key frame is counted WHEN ITS NAL UNIT IS ASSEMBLED, not when the
    access unit is delivered.  A key frame that arrives inside a damaged
    access unit is still a key frame the cloud sent us -- it just did not
    survive the reassembler.  Counting it at the delivery point would hide
    exactly the case we have to tell apart ("the cloud never sent a key
    frame" vs "we threw it away").

  * PeerVideoRtpStats (src/peer_connection.h) carries the four new
    fields; the two "ms" fields are AGES there ("how long ago", 0 = never),
    the absolute stamps stay in the decoder.

  * The diagnostic log carries the new counters:
      - the "rtp_h264 packets=... auOk=... auDrop=..." line now also shows
        "keyframes=... pliSent=...";
      - a new "pli_state sent=... keyframes=... mediaSsrc=...
        lastKeyframeAgoMs=... lastPliAgoMs=..." line every 2 s.

How the criterion changes (AGENTS.md 6.2, rule 8)
---------------------------------------------------

  * fault present (no PLI ever sent): videoKeyframesReceived = 0,
    videoPliSent = 0;
  * PLI contract v1 (stop after the first key frame): videoPliSent stops
    growing while a LATER packet loss corrupts the picture forever --
    videoLastKeyframeMs keeps growing and nothing heals it;
  * PLI contract v2 (B2, stale window): while the picture is healthy
    videoPliSent ticks up about once per LIBPEER_PLI_STALE_MS window and
    videoLastKeyframeMs stays small; after a loss burst videoPliSent
    grows faster until a fresh IDR arrives;
  * PLI fixed, the cloud does NOT answer: videoPliSent > 0 and
    videoKeyframesReceived = 0 -- that is the line that says "the cloud
    does not answer our key frame requests" (and the search has to go to
    the cloud side).

Every one of them is a counter that MOVES, so the report line is a
criterion and not a decoration.

A host-side self test of the rule itself (and of the source invariants this
patch establishes) is next to this file:
tools/libpeer-switch/0011-pli-trigger-check.mjs

Note for the Switch build
-------------------------

src/rtp.h changes the LAYOUT of struct RtpDecoder (four counters are
added) and src/peer_connection.h changes the LAYOUT of
PeerVideoRtpStats (four fields are added), and the engine links the
INSTALLED copy of libpeer (the headers under build/dist/include/) =>
libpeer.a HAS TO BE REBUILT and the installed headers have to be
refreshed, otherwise the engine sees a different structure than the one
libpeer allocates (that is not a clean compile error, it is memory
corruption).
